/-
Fmt/M1ProgPRFiles.lean: test data for a verifier, namely files of the first toy (a product test),
written by the programs, as bytes.

The first toy is a small test protocol that the programs (the C++ implementation that comes with
this Lean code) can run from end to end, writing the proof to a file. Its claim is that three rows
a, b, c of one oracle message have a_i b_i = c_i for every i (the heads of Fmt/M1Types.lean and
Fmt/M1Psc.lean). This file holds one such run as Lean data: the run at tiny parameters with a
repeated position. That run was made under another key of the prover's coins, at which one position
is drawn twice. The file holds the parameter bytes, the public file, the honest proof and 13 altered
copies of it. All data are made up: there is no model and there are no weights.

Sources. Made by a program from the files as they came, in the programs' archive of files (sha256
f97d6774317660e9033841ab36ce4e63e376ebeb25542cf6f1f92e8f61644aa1; the archive is not in this
repository). By the archive's README its files were made by a script of the programs' tests; that
script is not in this repository. No Mathlib is imported. Which file of the archive each name is:
params, pubs, proof: honest-and-altered/product/tiny-with-a-repeat/tiny.parameters, .configuration,
.proof; "altered-NN": honest-and-altered/product/tiny-with-a-repeat/tiny.altered-NN.proof.

1. What the file contains.
   params, pubs, proof    The parameter bytes, the public file of the configuration (a list of one),
                          and the honest proof file.
   f_altered_NN           The altered copies: the honest proof with some bytes changed, its last
                          byte cut off, or a zero byte added. The archive ships each copy as a file.
                          Here a copy is written as a function of the honest proof, to keep this
                          file small. The program that made this file found the function by
                          comparing the shipped copy with the proof, and it stops unless the
                          function gives back the shipped file byte for byte.
   table                  Name, bytes, and the part of the check at which the file is expected to
                          fail: 1 the form of the file, 2 the proof of work, 3 the Merkle openings,
                          4 the last block's linear check, 5 the last block's checks at the opened
                          positions; 0 means accepted. The honest proof is first, with 0. For an
                          altered copy the part is the one at which the programs' C++ verifier
                          refused it (read off its words in tiny.altered.tsv).
   refusedOnly            Files that must be refused but for which no single part is expected: a
                          copy that fails two parts, so that the part a verifier meets first depends
                          on the order of its checks.
   noWord                 Files about which the archive says nothing.

2. What is checked when the file compiles (tests, #guard). Every size. For every file, the altered
copies as made here among them, the format's hash under the number 168 (computed by HF of
Fmt/Rules5Fast.lean, which is proved to be the format's hash) against a value that the format page's
own reference program (format_rules.py) computed from the archive's file, at a moment when that
file's sha256 agreed with the archive's list of sums. So the bytes here can be compared with the
archive by a program. The three lists together have as many members as there are files.

3. Theorems. No theorem in this file. A test (#guard) is an evaluation made while the file compiles;
the compile fails if the result moves; it leaves no theorem.

4. What is not claimed. Nothing is said here about what any verifier in Lean answers on these files;
see Fmt/M1EnginePR.lean and the files that import this one.

Terms: lean/GLOSSARY.md.
-/
import Fmt.Rules5Fast

namespace Fmt
namespace M1
namespace ProgPR

def params : ByteArray := unhex ""

def pubs : List ByteArray := [unhex ""]

def proof : ByteArray := unhex ""

/-- the bytes b with the bytes w written over them from the place n on -/
def putAt (b : ByteArray) (n : Nat) (w : ByteArray) : ByteArray :=
  b.extract 0 n ++ w ++ b.extract (n + w.size) b.size

def f_altered_01 : ByteArray := putAt proof 0 (unhex "2e")

def f_altered_02 : ByteArray := putAt proof 32 (unhex "13")

def f_altered_03 : ByteArray := putAt proof 64 (unhex "5d")

def f_altered_04 : ByteArray := putAt proof 88 (unhex "da")

def f_altered_05 : ByteArray := putAt proof 112 (unhex "28")

def f_altered_06 : ByteArray := putAt proof 576 (unhex "84")

def f_altered_07 : ByteArray := putAt proof 2216 (unhex "f6")

def f_altered_08 : ByteArray := putAt proof 584 (unhex "07")

def f_altered_09 : ByteArray := putAt proof 656 (unhex "72")

def f_altered_10 : ByteArray := putAt proof 920 (unhex "28")

def f_altered_11 : ByteArray := putAt proof 584 (unhex "ffffffffffffffff")

def f_altered_12 : ByteArray := proof.extract 0 (proof.size - 1)

def f_altered_13 : ByteArray := proof.push 0

def table : List (String × ByteArray × Nat) :=
  [("proof", proof, 0), ("altered-01", f_altered_01, 2), ("altered-02", f_altered_02, 2),
    ("altered-03", f_altered_03, 2), ("altered-04", f_altered_04, 2),
    ("altered-05", f_altered_05, 2), ("altered-06", f_altered_06, 2),
    ("altered-08", f_altered_08, 3), ("altered-09", f_altered_09, 3),
    ("altered-10", f_altered_10, 3), ("altered-11", f_altered_11, 1),
    ("altered-12", f_altered_12, 1), ("altered-13", f_altered_13, 1)]

def refusedOnly : List (String × ByteArray) := [("altered-07", f_altered_07)]

def noWord : List (String × ByteArray) := []

def further : List (String × ByteArray) :=
  [("altered-01", f_altered_01), ("altered-02", f_altered_02), ("altered-03", f_altered_03),
    ("altered-04", f_altered_04), ("altered-05", f_altered_05), ("altered-06", f_altered_06),
    ("altered-07", f_altered_07), ("altered-08", f_altered_08), ("altered-09", f_altered_09),
    ("altered-10", f_altered_10), ("altered-11", f_altered_11), ("altered-12", f_altered_12),
    ("altered-13", f_altered_13)]

